<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Territory of Inini</h1><p class="st-meta">1930 CE – 1946 CE · capital Saint-Élie</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The vast forested interior of French Guiana, governed as a separate territory from 1930 to 1946 in an attempt to develop it apart from the coast.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1930 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> separated the interior of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-guiana">French Guiana</a> from the coastal colony, creating the Territory of Inini. The aim was to develop the gold-rich forests and administer the indigenous and maroon peoples directly, outside the control of the coastal elected council.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Inini was governed directly by the governor of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-guiana">French Guiana</a>, with its nominal capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-elie">Saint-Élie</a>, a gold-mining settlement. Gold prospecting was the main economic activity, and there was little infrastructure beyond river transport. The territory covered most of French Guiana's land but held only a small population of indigenous Amerindian peoples, maroon communities and gold miners.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1946, when <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-guiana">French Guiana</a> became an overseas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Inini was merged back into it, though the interior kept a separate administrative status until 1969. Its former area now forms the interior communes of French Guiana, home to Amerindian and maroon communities.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/territory-of-inini.jpg" alt="Territory of Inini" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1930 CE – 1946 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saint-Élie</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous religions, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Inini</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1938&amp;focus=territory-of-inini" aria-label="Territory of Inini on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3202 1997 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Territory of Inini lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3522 2253L3549 2249M3522 2253L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3549 2249h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3522 2253h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3522 2253h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Saint-Élie. <a href="/atlas?year=1938&amp;focus=territory-of-inini">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
